      "slug": "overview",
      "nav": "Overview",
      "group": "Getting started",
      "kind": "overview",
      "title": "Spis overview",
      "description": "What Spis is and how every record earns its place in the corpus.",
      "sections": [
        {
          "title": "What Spis is",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "claim": {
                "source": "reference-help",
                "evidence": "usage: spis <command> [tool flags]"
              },
              "text": "Spis is an evidence-grade reference corpus: 700 records across 13 interface families, each carrying its source, hashes, provenance class, and measured state."
            },
            {
              "type": "paragraph",
              "claim": {
                "source": "contract",
                "evidence": "Per-example completion floor"
              },
              "text": "A record exists only with its evidence. A missing observation is recorded as an evidence gap and is never promoted into prose."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "title": "Words this documentation uses",
          "blocks": [
            {
              "type": "bullets",
              "claim": {
                "source": "contract",
                "evidence": "provenance class"
              },
              "items": [
                "`record` \u2014 one measured reference: media, source URL, hashes, provenance class, and status.",
                "`provenance class` \u2014 how the media was obtained: `local-product-run`, `local-browser-run`, or `upstream-owner-media`.",
                "`evidence gap` \u2014 something the corpus does not know, recorded instead of guessed.",
                "`catalog` \u2014 one family of records, such as web-app-examples or cli-examples."
              ]
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
